drowsy driving could be responsible for  thousands more deaths on us roads every  year than first thought new research out  from triple-a overnight says drowsiness  could play a part in nearly ten percent  of all crashes in last month 29 percent  of drivers admit to being behind the  wheel when they had a hard time keeping  their eyes open Kris Van Cleave is on  the road in Washington with the dangers  Kris good morning well good morning  researchers and police have long  suspected that drowsy driving was an  underestimated underreported factor in  crashes now the CDC says the average  adult needs at least seven hours of  sleep at night many people don't get  that much sleep and here's where that  can be dangerous researchers have found  that if you're a drowsy driver on just  four hours of sleep you could be as  dangerous as a drunk driver when police  in Addison Illinois pulled over the  driver of this pickup he said he was  coming off a 14-hour shift and was  exhausted that driver got a warning  it just never dawned on me that we would  lose her but a drowsy driving crash  killed Jennifer Pierce's 18 year old  sister Nicole the Virginia Tech freshman  was coming back from a ski trip with  four friends when the driver fell asleep  and slammed into a tree for ten years  we've had to deal with her loss and not  just the loss of who she was at 18 but  the loss of the many years and who she  would have become this teen was asleep  behind the wheel when he rear-ended  another car no one was hurt triple-a  researchers studied videos like these  from cameras mounted in cars of 3500  drivers looking for signs of fatigue  federal estimates cite drowsiness as a  factor in one to two percent of crashes  when researchers analyzed more than 700  crashes caught on tape they found signs  of drowsy driving in roughly 10% one in  10 one in ten that's a lot of crashes  definitely William Horry is from the  triple-a foundation for traffic safety  there's really no truly effective  countermeasure to drowsiness as asleep  it really comes back to trying to plan  and give yourself enough time to get  that sleep now if that's seven to nine  hours of recommended sleep sounds like a  luxury some things to keep in mind  researchers say the most dangerous times  to drive drowsy or overnight and things  to look out for e if you're having  trouble maintaining your lane keeping  your eyes open or don't remember the  last few miles you've driven those could  all be signs you are too tired to be  behind the wheel go home and go to bed  seven to eight hours sounds like a dream
 

